WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Planning starts with the workload and the RAM budget. Count more than the base image.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.740
Include the kernel, initramfs, root image, writable overlay, file cache,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.560
desktop environment, graphics stack, browsers, containers, development tools,

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.860
package operations, and the applications the system actually runs.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.440
A design that looks fine immediately after boot may fail when a browser opens many tabs or

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.300
when a package update writes thousands of files.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.400
Next, decide what must persist. User data is the obvious category,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.620
but it is not the only one. Logs may be needed for troubleshooting or audit.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.960
Configuration may need to survive across boots. Package databases and updates may need a

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.460
maintenance path.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.240
SSH host keys, machine identity, VPN profiles, certificates, browser profiles,

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.660
application caches, containers, virtual machine images,

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.760
and local databases all need explicit decisions.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.860
If a value should survive, decide where and how. If it should not survive,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.680
document that too. Updates are often the hardest part.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.140
If the base system is read-only and changes land in a RAM overlay,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.000
ordinary package updates may disappear at reboot.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.860
That may be acceptable for a disposable live session, but not for a managed workstation.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.640
Possible strategies include rebuilding the base image, snapshotting a known-good state,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.020
maintaining a persistent writable layer, or applying updates in a maintenance boot.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.600
Each strategy has tradeoffs in complexity, rollback,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.620
and testing. Logs deserve special attention.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.040
RAM-only logs are useful for a clean reset,

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.700
but poor for diagnosing a failure that happened before reboot.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:48.440
Persistent local logs can consume storage and reveal activity. Remote logs can preserve

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.220
evidence without writing heavily to the local system.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.880
Choose intentionally. Measure under realistic conditions.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.820
Boot the system, run the expected workload, simulate updates, fill caches,

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.760
restart services, and watch memory pressure. Test shutdown and reboot.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:10.500
Confirm what persists and what resets. Document RAM assumptions, persistent mounts,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.280
update steps, and rollback steps.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.380
RAM-backed systems reward planning and punish guesses.
